WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.680 --> 00:00:02.880
If you want to understand the bind
the world faces

00:00:02.920 --> 00:00:06.080
adapting to climate change, there
may be no better example

00:00:06.120 --> 00:00:09.000
than that of the simple
air conditioner.

00:00:09.040 --> 00:00:11.960
As the world warms and heatwaves
become more frequent,

00:00:12.000 --> 00:00:14.880
and more dangerous,
people turn up the AC.

00:00:14.920 --> 00:00:16.920
It's very effective
at cooling buildings

00:00:16.960 --> 00:00:18.840
and the people inside them.

00:00:18.880 --> 00:00:22.040
But air conditioners are also
power-hungry appliances,

00:00:22.080 --> 00:00:23.880
with a small unit in a single room

00:00:23.920 --> 00:00:26.080
using more electricity
than four fridges.

00:00:30.400 --> 00:00:33.800
There are currently around
1.6 billion air conditioners

00:00:33.840 --> 00:00:37.560
in the world, and that number
is expected to triple by 2050,

00:00:37.600 --> 00:00:40.760
where 10 new units
will be installed every second

00:00:40.800 --> 00:00:42.720
for the next three decades.

00:00:42.760 --> 00:00:46.240
At that point, emissions from
powering all that air conditioning

00:00:46.280 --> 00:00:49.480
could be as high as 2 billion tonnes
of CO2 a year,

00:00:49.520 --> 00:00:52.160
about the same as India
produces now.

00:00:52.200 --> 00:00:56.520
This is the ever-accelerating
feedback loop of air conditioning.

00:00:56.560 --> 00:00:59.120
Higher temperatures lead
to more AC.

00:00:59.160 --> 00:01:01.560
More AC drives
ever higher temperatures,

00:01:01.600 --> 00:01:03.240
and while this cycle continues,

00:01:03.280 --> 00:01:06.880
people will suffer from
the ill effects of extreme heat.

00:01:06.920 --> 00:01:10.360
It isn't only about the immediate
danger of death or illness

00:01:10.400 --> 00:01:11.840
during a heatwave either.

00:01:11.880 --> 00:01:14.320
Higher temperatures can make
air quality worse

00:01:14.360 --> 00:01:16.200
and chronic conditions flare up.

00:01:16.240 --> 00:01:19.960
Some studies suggest our thinking
and decision-making is more sluggish

00:01:20.000 --> 00:01:21.320
when it's hotter.

00:01:21.360 --> 00:01:23.720
Extreme heat can leave people
poorer, too,

00:01:23.760 --> 00:01:25.120
as working hours shrink,

00:01:25.160 --> 00:01:28.560
and infrastructure, like roads
and power, become unreliable.

00:01:28.600 --> 00:01:31.720
One way to address the problem
is to simply build

00:01:31.760 --> 00:01:33.160
a better air conditioner.

00:01:33.200 --> 00:01:36.640
There are UN-backed initiatives
to improve the efficiency of AC,

00:01:36.680 --> 00:01:39.720
so it uses less power
and produces fewer pollutants.

00:01:39.760 --> 00:01:42.720
But rather than betting
on new or better tech,

00:01:42.760 --> 00:01:45.800
it's also possible to change our
approach to cooling and comfort.

00:01:45.840 --> 00:01:48.320
Humanity lived
without air conditioning

00:01:48.360 --> 00:01:50.080
for much of its history.

00:01:50.120 --> 00:01:52.880
Some of the most charming aspects
of ancient cities,

00:01:52.920 --> 00:01:56.040
from Mediterranean courtyards
to narrow city streets,

00:01:56.080 --> 00:01:59.080
are actually ingeniously engineered
cooling measures

00:01:59.120 --> 00:02:01.200
that either block out
direct sunlight

00:02:01.240 --> 00:02:04.440
or use cool stones and plants to
keep the nearby air pleasant

00:02:04.480 --> 00:02:06.400
throughout baking afternoons.

00:02:06.440 --> 00:02:09.320
The picturesque white villages
of southern Spain have partly

00:02:09.360 --> 00:02:12.400
been painted that way because light
colours absorb less heat.

00:02:12.440 --> 00:02:16.120
This often went alongside
ways of living that were adapted

00:02:16.160 --> 00:02:17.720
to the hot weather.

00:02:17.760 --> 00:02:19.320
Taking a siesta meant avoiding work

00:02:19.360 --> 00:02:21.360
during the hottest periods
of the day.

00:02:21.400 --> 00:02:23.520
Those who traditionally lived
in the desert

00:02:23.560 --> 00:02:26.320
wore looser, lighter clothing,
like robes.

00:02:26.360 --> 00:02:29.520
Placing a wet towel on the head
or neck is common in India.

00:02:29.560 --> 00:02:31.920
Simply ensuring you know of a cool
place to move to

00:02:31.960 --> 00:02:36.040
during intense heat, like a basement
or shaded room, can also help.

00:02:36.080 --> 00:02:40.160
Our predecessors also developed some
clever cooling technologies.

00:02:40.200 --> 00:02:43.920
Stepwells, an ancient technique
that builds small pools of water

00:02:43.960 --> 00:02:46.120
into a structure,
often deep underground,

00:02:46.160 --> 00:02:49.200
to cool adjacent areas have long
been common in India,

00:02:49.240 --> 00:02:50.720
while across the Middle East,

00:02:50.760 --> 00:02:52.840
striking towers called
wind-catchers

00:02:52.880 --> 00:02:55.040
that direct cool air towards
buildings

00:02:55.080 --> 00:02:57.040
have been used for centuries.

00:02:57.080 --> 00:02:59.800
More recently, people have relied
on electric fans

00:02:59.840 --> 00:03:01.160
to circulate cool air,

00:03:01.200 --> 00:03:03.160
which use very little electricity.

00:03:03.200 --> 00:03:05.600
Including efficient ceiling fans
in buildings

00:03:05.640 --> 00:03:09.520
would go a long way to reducing the
quantity of air conditioning used.

00:03:09.560 --> 00:03:12.600
Many of these approaches are called
passive cooling measures

00:03:12.640 --> 00:03:14.960
because they don't consume energy
but, in a sense,

00:03:15.000 --> 00:03:18.600
air conditioning created a different
kind of passivity.

00:03:18.640 --> 00:03:21.640
It allowed people to design
frighteningly inefficient buildings

00:03:21.680 --> 00:03:25.720
and cities full of sealed all-glass
skyscrapers and concrete surfaces,

00:03:25.760 --> 00:03:27.880
and simply cool them down with AC.

00:03:27.920 --> 00:03:30.800
Reversing this mistake
for a warming world

00:03:30.840 --> 00:03:32.720
requires an active approach -

00:03:32.760 --> 00:03:34.920
designing buildings, cities

00:03:34.960 --> 00:03:37.400
and reconfiguring our daily lives
to ensure

00:03:37.440 --> 00:03:39.880
we can deal with overheating
before it happens,

00:03:39.920 --> 00:03:43.360
and treat air conditioning as
a last resort.

00:03:43.400 --> 00:03:47.080
Fortunately, greener, more carefully
built spaces for living

00:03:47.120 --> 00:03:49.920
should have other benefits -
from the savings that come

00:03:49.960 --> 00:03:53.160
with efficient homes to the health
and wellbeing effects of plentiful

00:03:53.200 --> 00:03:56.920
green space and safer outdoor
temperatures in towns and cities.

00:03:56.960 --> 00:03:59.360
Like the climate crisis itself,

00:03:59.400 --> 00:04:02.120
cooling is a global challenge.

00:04:02.160 --> 00:04:05.920
An Oxford University study showed
that if average global temperatures

00:04:05.960 --> 00:04:09.640
missed the 1.5 degree target set
by the Paris Agreement

00:04:09.680 --> 00:04:11.400
and hit 2 degrees,

00:04:11.440 --> 00:04:15.360
nations already facing heatwaves,
like those in Central Africa,

00:04:15.400 --> 00:04:17.800
will see the most extreme
temperatures.

00:04:17.840 --> 00:04:20.400
But northern countries could see
the biggest jump

00:04:20.440 --> 00:04:23.800
in the number of uncomfortably
hot days - days when people

00:04:23.840 --> 00:04:25.560
will need additional cooling.

00:04:25.600 --> 00:04:27.600
In Switzerland and the UK,
for instance,

00:04:27.640 --> 00:04:31.800
that number will go up by 30%,
the biggest increase globally.

00:04:31.840 --> 00:04:33.960
The researchers warned that
these countries

00:04:34.000 --> 00:04:37.280
are dangerously unprepared,
with their homes and cities

00:04:37.320 --> 00:04:40.120
built with a very different
climate in mind.

00:04:40.160 --> 00:04:43.440
In the UK, homes are built
"like greenhouses",

00:04:43.480 --> 00:04:45.880
one of the lead researchers said.

00:04:45.920 --> 00:04:48.760
Air conditioning is incredibly
convenient,

00:04:48.800 --> 00:04:51.640
but it would be a disaster
for the climate if it continues

00:04:51.680 --> 00:04:54.200
to be the worldwide remedy
for rising temperatures.

00:04:54.240 --> 00:04:56.560
The goal must be
to turn air conditioning

00:04:56.600 --> 00:05:00.240
into a technology of last resort,
using the clever green solutions

00:05:00.280 --> 00:05:03.800
of the past, and adapting the way
we live and work in a hotter world.

